    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-12674",
      "problem": "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$2^n > 2n$ for all $n \\geq 3, n\\in\\mathbb N$\n\nWhat are the missing entries in the proof template outlined below?\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14118159/assets/55b1aa9ddb93f56c2237d8c47d8c4e74126c4f287690fa23f2142a4e926a77c6.svg)\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14118159/assets/f4a96cfa3b106bf988ce2c42346c1654863fe23d0e2db674e4273145c70d2bd2.svg)\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14118159/assets/d173450199d3b813b8260082f81b4030333e9e1b0e1949b8972c95a17428162b.svg) We have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14118159/assets/5b2ed3cdec9017722c5123b54514d3f08787950c90d5b9e8993f45bd0f5fcbe2.svg)\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14118159/assets/858c9bbe6b754ef2f70be8a99babad3df452c10cb824f30fded9aa9b8b05189f.svg)\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, $2^n > 2n$ for all $n \\geq 3.$",
      "worked_solution": "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n2^n > 2n\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called **base case**. Since we are required to prove the statement for $n \\geq 3,$ the base case is $P(3).$ So, we substitute $n=3$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}n=3}.$\n\n$$\n\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2^3} > \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2(3)}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show that the inequality holds.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n = k \\geq 3.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}k \\geq 3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2^k}  > \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2k}\n$$\n\nThen, we move on to the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2^{k+1}} > \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2(k+1)}.\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this inequality using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is greater than the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, $2^n > 2n$ for all $n \\geq 3.$",
